= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Group the <polynomial> into two pairs, then use the <difference of two squares>:
$$
x^3-x^2-x+1=x^2(x-1)-(x-1)
=(x-1)(x^2-1)=\boxed{(x-1)^2(x+1).}
$$
Thus $1$ is a double <polynomial root> and $-1$ is a simple <polynomial root>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The repeated factor requires both a simple-pole and a double-pole term in the <partial fraction decomposition>. Write
$$
\frac1{(x-1)^2(x+1)}=\frac A{x+1}+\frac B{x-1}+\frac C{(x-1)^2}.
$$
Multiplying through gives $1=A(x-1)^2+B(x^2-1)+C(x+1)$. Substituting $x=-1$ and $x=1$ gives $A=1/4$ and $C=1/2$; the quadratic coefficient then gives $B=-1/4$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac1{x^3-x^2-x+1}
=\frac1{4(x+1)}-\frac1{4(x-1)}+\frac1{2(x-1)^2},\qquad x\ne\pm1.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a single point of contact the line is a <tangent line>, so its slope equals the <derivative> of the <exponential function>. At the contact coordinate $x_0$,
$$
m=e^{x_0},\qquad mx_0=e^{x_0}.
$$
Since $m>0$, dividing gives $x_0=1$, hence **$m=e$**. To verify uniqueness, the difference $e^x-ex$ has positive second <derivative> everywhere and its unique minimum is zero at $x=1$. Thus this line touches the curve once.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Using the contact coordinate $x_0=1$ and substituting into the <exponential function> gives
$$
\boxed{(x_0,y_0)=(1,e).}
$$
Both the curve and the line with the slope found in part (a) pass through this point.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Use <completing the square> to obtain
$$
(x-1)^2+y^2=4.
$$
The <locus> is therefore a **<circle> with <centre> $(1,0)$ and <radius> $2$**. It meets the horizontal axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(3,0)$ and the vertical axis at $(0,\pm\sqrt3)$; its highest and lowest points are $(1,\pm2)$.

\Image[paper-1-circle-locus.png]
{title=The circle locus, its centre, and its coordinate-axis intersections}

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On any interval where $\sin x>0$, the <chain rule> gives $y'=\cos x/\sin x=\cot x$. Differentiating again,
$$
\boxed{y''=-\csc^2x=-\frac1{\sin^2x}.}
$$
The domain condition is necessary because the real <natural logarithm> requires a positive argument.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Within the requested interval, the <function> is symmetric about $x=\pi/2$ because $\sin(\pi-x)=\sin x$. Its <derivative> $\cot x$ is positive before $\pi/2$, zero there, and negative afterwards. The negative second <derivative> shows that this is a strictly <concave function>. Consequently its **unique maximum is $(\pi/2,0)$**. It tends to $-\infty$ at both ends, giving vertical <asymptotes> **$x=0$ and $x=\pi$**.

\Image[paper-1-log-sine.png]
{title=The logarithm of sine between its two vertical asymptotes}

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For the <indefinite integral>, substitute $u=x^2$, so $x^3dx=u\,du/2$. An <integration by parts> then gives
$$
\int x^3\cos(x^2)dx=\frac12\int u\cos u\,du
=\boxed{\frac12\big[x^2\sin(x^2)+\cos(x^2)\big]+C.}
$$
For the <definite integral>, the original PDF's upper limit is $\sqrt{\pi/2}$, so the corresponding upper value of $u$ is $\pi/2$. Therefore
$$
\int_0^{\sqrt{\pi/2}}x^3\cos(x^2)dx
=\frac12\left[\frac\pi2\sin\frac\pi2+\cos\frac\pi2-1\right]
=\boxed{\frac{\pi-2}{4}.}
$$
Both requested integrals are thus evaluated with the same <antiderivative>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The maximum of <sine> is one. The allowed parameter values giving $\sin(3t)=1$ are
$$
t=\frac\pi6,\quad\frac{5\pi}6,\quad\frac{3\pi}2.
$$
Substituting each into $x=\cos t$ gives
$$
\boxed{y_{\max}=1,\qquad x\in\left\{\frac{\sqrt3}{2},-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},0\right\}.}
$$
All three parameter values lie in the original interval, so none of these maxima is discarded.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For this <parametric curve>, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{dx}{dt}=-\sin t,\qquad \frac{dy}{dt}=3\cos(3t),\qquad
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{3\cos(3t)}{\sin t}.}
$$
The quotient is a finite slope when $\sin t\ne0$. At $t=0$ and $t=\pi$, the horizontal component of the tangent vanishes while the vertical component does not; these have vertical <tangent lines>, rather than missing finite values of the displayed quotient.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <binomial series> with exponent $1/2$ and argument $-3x$:
$$
(1-3x)^{1/2}=1+\frac12(-3x)
+\frac{(1/2)(-1/2)}{2!}(-3x)^2+O(x^3).
$$
Thus the first three nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{1-\frac32x-\frac98x^2+O(x^3).}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The usual interior condition for the <binomial series> is $|3x|<1$, giving **$-1/3<x<1/3$** as its open interval of convergence. In this particular square-root case the full series also converges absolutely at both endpoints, so the maximal real range on which its sum equals the original <function> is
$$
\boxed{-\frac13\leq x\leq\frac13.}
$$
For an endpoint check, write $\sqrt{1-u}=1-\sum_{n\geq1}b_nu^n$, where $b_n=a_{n-1}-a_n>0$ and $a_n=\binom{2n}{n}/4^n\to0$. Then $\sum b_n=1$ by telescoping, establishing <absolute convergence> at $u=\pm1$. The <square-root binomial series at its endpoints> proves <continuity> of the sum there. The endpoint values are $\sqrt2$ at $x=-1/3$ and $0$ at $x=1/3$. Outside this range the power-series terms do not tend to zero. A three-term truncation remains an approximation, rather than an exact formula throughout the interval.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

On the specified domain $\sin x\ne0$, so multiplying by $\sin x$ is legitimate. Use the double-angle identity to find
$$
\cos x=2\sin^2x\cos x,
\qquad \cos x\,(1-2\sin^2x)=0.
$$
The first factor gives $x=\pm\pi/2$. The second gives $\sin^2x=1/2$, yielding four more values in the interval. Thus
$$
\boxed{x\in\left\{-\frac{3\pi}{4},-\frac\pi2,-\frac\pi4,
\frac\pi4,\frac\pi2,\frac{3\pi}{4}\right\}.}
$$
Every listed value has nonzero <sine> and satisfies the original equation. Dividing by $\cos x$ would incorrectly lose the two solutions $\pm\pi/2$.

