= Solution
{parent=a}

The positive horizontal coordinate is $x=\sqrt{3/2}=\sqrt6/2$. By <implicit differentiation>,
$$
2y\frac{dy}{dx}+4x=0,
\qquad \frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{2x}{y}.
$$
At the selected point this gives **$dy/dx=-\sqrt6$**.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <normal line to a plane curve> is perpendicular to the <tangent line>, so its slope is the negative reciprocal $1/\sqrt6$. The required equation is
$$
\boxed{y-1=\frac1{\sqrt6}\left(x-\frac{\sqrt6}{2}\right),
\qquad\text{equivalently}\quad y=\frac{x}{\sqrt6}+\frac12.}
$$
The normal direction also agrees with the <gradient> $(4x,2y)$ of the defining level <function>.

