= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The two coefficients are the corresponding <partial derivatives> of $e^{x+y}$. Therefore the <differential form> is exact, with
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=e^{x+y}+C,\qquad df=e^{x+y}dx+e^{x+y}dy.}
$$
The arbitrary constant does not change the <total differential>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a smooth <exact differential form> $Mdx+Ndy$, its coefficients must satisfy $M_y=N_x$ by <Clairaut's theorem>. Here
$$
M_y=\sin x\cos y,\qquad N_x=-\sin x\cos y.
$$
These are not equal as <functions> on the plane, so **the form is not exact**. Equality at isolated points or along some lines is insufficient: an exactness test must hold throughout an open domain.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The coefficients follow by differentiating the same monomial:
$$
d(x^2y^3z^4)=2xy^3z^4dx+3x^2y^2z^4dy+4x^2y^3z^3dz.
$$
Thus this <exact differential form> has
$$
\boxed{f(x,y,z)=x^2y^3z^4+C.}
$$
Exhibiting a global <scalar potential> proves exactness directly, without needing an additional domain argument.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <partial derivatives> are $g_x=\sin x$ and $g_y=y$, so all <stationary points> are $(n\pi,0)$ for integers $n$. Their <Hessian matrix> is $\operatorname{diag}(\cos x,1)$. At even multiples of $\pi$ it is a <positive-definite matrix>, whereas at odd multiples it has one positive and one negative <eigenvalue>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{(2n\pi,0):\text{ strict minima, }g=0;\qquad
((2n+1)\pi,0):\text{ saddle points, }g=2.}
$$
There are no <local maxima>. Since both $1-\cos x$ and $y^2/2$ are nonnegative, these are also <global minima>. In the requested open plotting window, the <stationary points> are $(0,0)$ and $(\pm\pi,0)$; the <local minima> at $(\pm2\pi,0)$ lie on excluded edges.

For a <level set> $g=c$, solving for the vertical coordinate gives
$$
y=\pm\sqrt{2(c-1+\cos x)},\qquad c\geq1-\cos x.
$$
For $0<c<2$ these <level sets> are closed ovals around each <local minimum>. At $c=0$ the <level sets> shrink to the <local minima>. The <level set> through the <saddle points> is
$$
g=2:\qquad y=\pm2\cos(x/2),
$$
where the two signed branches together describe the same set as $y=\pm2|\cos(x/2)|$. They cross at $(\pm\pi,0)$ and reach $(0,\pm2)$. The crossing slopes at a <saddle point> are $\pm1$, as also follows from the local <quadratic form> $(-\delta x^2+\delta y^2)/2$. For $c>2$, the upper and lower <level sets> remain separate and extend across the plotting window without meeting the horizontal axis.

\Image[paper-1-cosine-contours.png]
{title=Contours of the cosine-plus-quadratic function, with its saddle level and stationary points labelled}

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Set $T=t+a>0$. The <similarity solution> uses $y=(x+b)T^{-1/2}$, whose <partial derivatives> at fixed $x$ or $t$ are
$$
y_x=T^{-1/2},\qquad y_t=-\frac y{2T}.
$$
Apply the <multivariable chain rule> to $u=T^{-1/2}v(y)$:
$$
u_t=-\frac12T^{-3/2}v+T^{-1/2}v'\left(-\frac y{2T}\right)
=-\frac12T^{-3/2}(v+yv'),
$$
$$
u_x=T^{-1}v',\qquad u_{xx}=T^{-3/2}v''.
$$
Substituting in the <heat equation> and cancelling the positive common factor $T^{-3/2}$ gives
$$
\boxed{-\frac12(v+yv')=\lambda v''.}
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <Gaussian function>, the required <derivatives> are
$$
v'=-2cyv,\qquad v''=(-2c+4c^2y^2)v.
$$
Substitute these into the reduced <ordinary differential equation> and compare the constant and quadratic coefficients:
$$
-\frac12+cy^2=\lambda(-2c+4c^2y^2).
$$
The constant terms give $c=1/(4\lambda)$, and this value also satisfies the quadratic terms. Hence
$$
\boxed{c=\frac1{4\lambda},\qquad
u(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt{t+a}}
\exp\left[-\frac{(x+b)^2}{4\lambda(t+a)}\right],\qquad t>-a.}
$$
A constant multiple is also a solution by <linearity>.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <heat equation> is linear and homogeneous in $u$: the operator $L=\partial_t-\lambda\partial_x^2$ satisfies $L(Au_1+Bu_2)=ALu_1+BLu_2$. Thus the <superposition principle> applies to its solutions. Spatial translations are also allowed because the coefficients are constant.

Choose $a=1/(4\lambda)$ in part (b) and multiply by $\sqrt a$ to make the initial amplitude one. Taking the two shifts $b=1$ and $b=-1$ then gives
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt{1+4\lambda t}}
\left\{\exp\left[-\frac{(x+1)^2}{1+4\lambda t}\right]
+\exp\left[-\frac{(x-1)^2}{1+4\lambda t}\right]\right\}.}
$$
At $t=0$ this reproduces both initial peaks. Each term is an instance of <Gaussian initial data for the heat equation>, so their sum satisfies the equation. Its extension to negative times is a real smooth solution precisely on the connected interval
$$
\boxed{-\frac1{4\lambda}<t<0.}
$$
At the lower endpoint the peaks narrow without bound and their heights diverge; there is no finite classical continuation there. For positive times each peak spreads and decreases in height while preserving its integral.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiate the <cycloid> parametrization:
$$
\boxed{\frac{dx}{dt}=a(1-\cos t),\qquad
\frac{dy}{dt}=a\sin t,\qquad
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{\sin t}{1-\cos t}=\cot\frac t2.}
$$
The half-angle simplification is valid throughout $0<t<2\pi$, where $1-\cos t>0$. In particular the horizontal coordinate increases strictly along the arch.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <cycloid> has limiting endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2\pi a,0)$, with its highest point $(\pi a,2a)$ at $t=\pi$. The transformation $t\mapsto2\pi-t$ sends $x$ to $2\pi a-x$ and preserves $y$, so the arch is symmetric about $x=\pi a$. Its slope decreases from $+\infty$ through zero to $-\infty$, giving vertical limiting <tangent lines> at the cusps and a horizontal <tangent line> at the top. More explicitly,
$$
\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=-\frac1{4a\sin^4(t/2)}<0,
$$
so the arch is a <concave function> throughout its interior. The endpoint cusps are limiting points, excluded by the open parameter interval.

\Image[paper-1-cycloid-arch.png]
{title=One cycloid arch, its limiting cusps, highest point, and the point with slope square root of three}

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The original PDF specifies slope $\sqrt3$; the transcription's $\sqrt{-1}$ is erroneous. By part (a), $\cot(t/2)=\sqrt3$. Since $t/2\in(0,\pi)$ and <cotangent> is strictly decreasing there, the unique parameter value is $t=\pi/3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(a\left(\frac\pi3-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right),\frac a2\right).}
$$
The <tangent line> drawn in the preceding figure has the required positive slope.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Because $dx/dt>0$ along the arch, its area above the baseline is the <definite integral> $\int y\,dx$. Changing to the parameter gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A
&=a^2\int_0^{2\pi}(1-\cos t)^2dt\\
&=a^2\left[\int_0^{2\pi}1\,dt-2\int_0^{2\pi}\cos t\,dt
+\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2t\,dt\right]\\
&=a^2(2\pi+\pi)=\boxed{3\pi a^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Adding or removing the two endpoint points does not change the area. This is the area formula in <area and length of a cycloid arch>.

= e
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <arc length> follows from the <speed> of the parametrization:
$$
\sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2+\left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2}
=a\sqrt{2-2\cos t}=2a|\sin(t/2)|.
$$
Here $\sin(t/2)>0$ inside the given interval. Therefore
$$
\boxed{L=2a\int_0^{2\pi}\sin(t/2)dt=8a.}
$$
The result scales as a length, while the preceding area scales as $a^2$, as required by <dimensional analysis>.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $n\geq3$, <Taylor's theorem> after $n$ terms, counting the constant term, is
$$
f(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2
+\cdots+\frac{f^{(n-1)}(a)}{(n-1)!}(x-a)^{n-1}+R_n(x).
$$
Under the usual hypothesis that $f$ is $n-1$ times continuously differentiable on the interval joining $a$ and $x$, and $n$ times differentiable in its interior, the <Taylor theorem with Lagrange remainder> gives
$$
\boxed{R_n(x)=\frac{f^{(n)}(\xi)}{n!}(x-a)^n,
\qquad \xi\text{ strictly between }a\text{ and }x.}
$$
This is a finite expansion with a controlled remainder; it does not assume an infinite <Taylor series> converges. For $x=a$ the remainder is zero.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $f(x)=\cosh\sqrt x$ near $x=2$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
f'(x)=\frac{\sinh\sqrt x}{2\sqrt x},\qquad
f''(x)=\frac{\cosh\sqrt x}{4x}-\frac{\sinh\sqrt x}{4x^{3/2}}.
$$
Substitute $x=2$ into the first three terms of the <Taylor expansion>:
$$
\boxed{\cosh\sqrt x
=\cosh\sqrt2+\frac{\sinh\sqrt2}{2\sqrt2}(x-2)
+\left(\frac{\cosh\sqrt2}{16}-\frac{\sinh\sqrt2}{16\sqrt2}\right)(x-2)^2
+O((x-2)^3).}
$$
The quadratic coefficient is $f''(2)/2$, not $f''(2)$. The apparent square root creates no local difficulty at the positive expansion point.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <Maclaurin series> $\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)$ and $(1+x)^{-2}=1-2x+3x^2+O(x^3)$. Multiplying through the cubic order gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sin x}{(1+x)^2}
=x-2x^2+\frac{17}{6}x^3+O(x^4).}
$$
These are the first three nonzero terms; the constant term vanishes.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Both numerator and denominator vanish quadratically. Factor out $x^2$ before dividing the <power series>:
$$
\frac{x\sin x}{\ln(1+x^2)}
=\frac{1-x^2/6+x^4/120+O(x^6)}{1-x^2/2+x^4/3+O(x^6)}.
$$
The reciprocal of the denominator is $1+x^2/2-x^4/12+O(x^6)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{x\sin x}{\ln(1+x^2)}
=1+\frac{x^2}{3}-\frac{19x^4}{120}+O(x^6).}
$$
The expansion describes the continuous extension at the <removable singularity> $x=0$, where the original quotient is undefined but its limit is one. Odd powers vanish because the quotient is even.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Uniform <sampling without replacement> is equivalent to revealing a uniformly random <permutation> of seven labelled balls. Each draw <position> is equally likely to contain any label, so its colour has the original proportions. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(W_1)=\frac37,\qquad
\mathbb P(B_2)=\frac47,\qquad
\mathbb P(W_3)=\frac37.}
$$
For example the <law of total probability> gives $\mathbb P(B_2)=(4/7)(3/6)+(3/7)(4/6)=4/7$. Equal marginal <probabilities> express <exchangeability>, rather than <independence> between successive draws.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The first black draw has <probability> $4/7$. Given that event, three of the remaining six balls are black. The multiplication rule therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_1\cap B_2)=\frac47\frac36=\frac27.}
$$
Using $(4/7)^2$ would incorrectly treat the draws as independent.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Any two distinct draw positions have the same pair law by <exchangeability>. The <marginal and pair probabilities in sampling without replacement> therefore give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_2\cap B_3)=\frac{4\cdot3}{7\cdot6}=\frac27.}
$$
Divide this joint <probability> by the relevant marginal <probability> to get the two <conditional probabilities>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_2\mid B_3)=\frac{2/7}{4/7}=\frac12,\qquad
\mathbb P(B_3\mid B_2)=\frac{2/7}{4/7}=\frac12.}
$$
Conditioning on the third draw can change the <probability> of the second draw; <conditional probability> does not require the conditioning event to occur earlier in time.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Use the <inclusion-exclusion principle> for two events:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_1\cup B_2)
=\frac47+\frac47-\frac27=\frac67.}
$$
As a check, its complement is two white draws, with <probability> $(3/7)(2/6)=1/7$.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Let $I_j$ be the <indicator random variable> of a black ball at draw $j$. The black count after three draws is $N(B_3)=I_1+I_2+I_3$. By <linearity of expectation>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[N(B_3)]=\sum_{j=1}^3\mathbb E[I_j]
=3\frac47=\frac{12}{7}.}
$$
<Independence> is not required for this step. This is also the <mean> of the corresponding <hypergeometric distribution>.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Distinguish the $N$ balls by labels. For a fixed <position> $n$, each label appears there in exactly $(N-1)!$ of the $N!$ equally likely <permutations>. Since $N_B$ labels are black,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_n)=\frac{N_B(N-1)!}{N!}=\frac{N_B}{N},
\qquad 1\leq n\leq N.}
$$
This is the same marginal law at every <position>, even the last one. The argument averages over earlier draw outcomes; it does not assert that a <conditional probability> after a specified history remains $N_B/N$. The original PDF uses $B_n$, correcting the transcription's uppercase subscript $B_N$.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $b>0$, split the interval into $n$ equal widths $\Delta x=b/n$. Since $x^2$ increases on this interval, the left and right rectangles give lower and upper <Riemann sums>:
$$
L_n=\frac{b^3}{n^3}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}k^2,
\qquad
U_n=\frac{b^3}{n^3}\sum_{k=1}^{n}k^2.
$$
Using the finite sum of squares,
$$
U_n=\frac{b^3n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6n^3}\longrightarrow\frac{b^3}{3}.
$$
The rectangle bounds differ by $U_n-L_n=b^3/n\to0$. The <squeeze theorem> therefore gives, directly from elementary areas,
$$
\boxed{\int_0^b x^2dx=\frac{b^3}{3}.}
$$
For $b=0$ the result is zero. If negative $b$ is allowed, reverse the <integration> limits and use the reflection $x=-u$; the same signed formula holds. No <antiderivative> is needed for this first-principles calculation.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $x=2\sin\theta$ with $-\pi/2<\theta<\pi/2$, so $dx=2\cos\theta\,d\theta$ and $\sqrt{4-x^2}=2\cos\theta$. The <integration by substitution> gives
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{dx}{\sqrt{4-x^2}}=\arcsin\frac x2+C,
\qquad -2<x<2.}
$$
This uses the principal <inverse sine>; the chosen angle interval keeps the square root positive.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Complete the square in the denominator and split the numerator into a multiple of its <derivative> plus a constant:
$$
x^2+4x+8=(x+2)^2+4,\qquad x+1=\frac12(2x+4)-1.
$$
Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int\frac{x+1}{x^2+4x+8}dx
&=\frac12\int\frac{2x+4}{x^2+4x+8}dx
-\int\frac{dx}{(x+2)^2+4}\\
&=\boxed{\frac12\ln(x^2+4x+8)
-\frac12\arctan\frac{x+2}{2}+C.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The <natural logarithm> needs no absolute-value correction because its quadratic argument is strictly positive for every real $x$. The second term is an <inverse tangent> <antiderivative>.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First apply <integration by parts> with $u=x^k$ and $dv=\sin x\,dx$:
$$
F(k)=\big[-x^k\cos x\big]_0^\pi
+k\int_0^\pi x^{k-1}\cos x\,dx
=\pi^k+k\int_0^\pi x^{k-1}\cos x\,dx.
$$
For $k>1$, integrate the remaining integral by parts once more. Its boundary term $[x^{k-1}\sin x]_0^\pi$ is zero, so
$$
\boxed{F(k)=\pi^k-k(k-1)F(k-2).}
$$
At $k=1$, direct <integration> gives $F(1)=\pi$, agreeing with the same formula; the coefficient of $F(-1)$ vanishes and that integral is finite. Now follow the odd chain:
$$
F(3)=\pi^3-6\pi,\qquad
\boxed{F(5)=\pi^5-20\pi^3+120\pi.}
$$
This is the <sine-weighted power integral recurrence>.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\mathbf e_1,\mathbf e_2,\mathbf e_3$ be the Cartesian <basis vectors>, with the given <vector> $\mathbf x=\mathbf e_1$. The <permutation matrix> cycles them forward:
$$
\boxed{A\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
A^2\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
A^3\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Thus three applications return the original <vector>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Interchanging the rows and columns gives the <matrix transpose>
$$
\boxed{A^{\mathsf T}=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&0&1\\1&0&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
It cycles the three <basis vectors> in the opposite direction. Hence
$$
\boxed{A^{\mathsf T}\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{pmatrix},\quad
(A^{\mathsf T})^2\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{pmatrix},\quad
(A^{\mathsf T})^3\mathbf x=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The preceding cycles show the action on a full <basis>, not only on one direction: $A$ sends $\mathbf e_1\to\mathbf e_2\to\mathbf e_3\to\mathbf e_1$, and $A^{\mathsf T}$ reverses each arrow. Therefore either composition fixes every <basis vector>, and by <linearity> fixes every <vector>. Thus
$$
\boxed{A^{\mathsf T}A=AA^{\mathsf T}=I.}
$$
In particular $A$ is an <orthogonal matrix>, $A^{\mathsf T}=A^{-1}=A^2$, and $A^3=I$.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For an <eigenvector> $\mathbf v=(v_1,v_2,v_3)^{\mathsf T}$, the equation $A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v$ reads
$$
v_3=\lambda v_1,\qquad v_1=\lambda v_2,\qquad v_2=\lambda v_3.
$$
A nonzero solution requires $\lambda^3=1$. Write the three <roots of unity> in the requested <polar form of a complex number>:
$$
\boxed{\lambda_k=e^{2\pi ik/3},\qquad
\mathbf v_k=\begin{pmatrix}1\\e^{4\pi ik/3}\\e^{2\pi ik/3}\end{pmatrix},
\qquad k=0,1,2.}
$$
Indeed $\mathbf v_k=(1,\lambda_k^2,\lambda_k)^{\mathsf T}$ satisfies all three equations. These <eigenvectors> are unnormalized; any nonzero scalar multiples are equally valid.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Since $A^{\mathsf T}=A^{-1}$, each <eigenvector> $\mathbf v_k$ from part (d) remains an <eigenvector>, with reciprocal <eigenvalue>:
$$
\boxed{\mu_k=\lambda_k^{-1}=e^{-2\pi ik/3},\qquad
A^{\mathsf T}\mathbf v_k=\mu_k\mathbf v_k.}
$$
The set of <eigenvalues> is again $\{1,e^{2\pi i/3},e^{4\pi i/3}\}$, with the two nonreal values exchanged relative to the same <eigenvectors>. Equivalently, for $\mu=e^{2\pi ik/3}$ one can choose the <eigenvector> $(1,\mu,\mu^2)^{\mathsf T}$. Pairing each <eigenvalue> with its <eigenvector> matters in the next part.

= f
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The <symmetric part of a matrix> is
$$
\boxed{S=\frac12(A+A^{\mathsf T}).}
$$
It satisfies $S^{\mathsf T}=S$. The complementary term $(A-A^{\mathsf T})/2$ is a <skew-symmetric matrix>.

= g
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

Use the common <eigenvectors> of $A$ and $A^{\mathsf T}$. For each $k$,
$$
S\mathbf v_k=\frac12(\lambda_k+\lambda_k^{-1})\mathbf v_k
=\cos\frac{2\pi k}{3}\,\mathbf v_k.
$$
Thus, without constructing $S$ explicitly, its <eigenvalues> are
$$
\boxed{1,\ -\frac12,\ -\frac12.}
$$
The <eigenvalue> $1$ has direction $(1,1,1)^{\mathsf T}$. The repeated <eigenvalue> $-1/2$ has a real <eigenspace> consisting of <vectors> whose coordinates sum to zero. This is the general rule for the <symmetric part of an orthogonal matrix>. Adding unrelated lists of <eigenvalues> would not suffice; here the shared <eigenvectors> justify the pairing.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $r\ne1$, differentiate the <finite geometric series> and multiply by $r$:
$$
\sum_{n=1}^Nnr^n
=r\frac d{dr}\left(\frac{1-r^{N+1}}{1-r}\right)
=\frac{r[1-(N+1)r^N+Nr^{N+1}]}{(1-r)^2}.
$$
The required terms are $-nr^n$ with $r=-1/3$. The <finite weighted geometric sum> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{S_N=\frac3{16}\left[1-\left(1+\frac{4N}{3}\right)
\left(-\frac13\right)^N\right].}
$$
Since $N/3^N\to0$, the correction tends to zero, proving convergence and yielding
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n}{3^n}=\frac3{16}.}
$$
In fact the <ratio test> also proves <absolute convergence>, since the ratio of consecutive absolute terms tends to $1/3$.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By <De Moivre's theorem>, $(e^{i\alpha})^j=e^{ij\alpha}$. The complex sum whose real part is wanted is therefore a <finite geometric series>:
$$
Z=\sum_{j=0}^ne^{i(\theta+j\alpha)}
=e^{i\theta}\frac{1-e^{i(n+1)\alpha}}{1-e^{i\alpha}},
\qquad \alpha\notin2\pi\mathbb Z.
$$
Use $1-e^{i\beta}=-2ie^{i\beta/2}\sin(\beta/2)$ in the numerator and denominator. This yields
$$
Z=\frac{\sin((n+1)\alpha/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}
e^{i(\theta+n\alpha/2)}.
$$
Taking its real part proves the <finite trigonometric sum>
$$
\boxed{\sum_{j=0}^n\cos(\theta+j\alpha)
=\frac{\sin((n+1)\alpha/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}
\cos\left(\theta+\frac{n\alpha}{2}\right).}
$$
When $\alpha\in2\pi\mathbb Z$, the printed quotient is undefined; the original sum is directly **$(n+1)\cos\theta$**, also the limiting value of the displayed identity. The denominator restriction cannot be omitted from a literal use of the quotient.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $a_n=\ln[1+(-1)^n/n]$ and $S_N=\sum_{n=2}^Na_n$. Consecutive even and odd terms cancel exactly:
$$
a_{2j}=\ln\frac{2j+1}{2j},\qquad
a_{2j+1}=\ln\frac{2j}{2j+1}=-a_{2j}.
$$
Thus
$$
S_{2m+1}=0,\qquad S_{2m}=\ln\frac{2m+1}{2m}\longrightarrow0.
$$
Both parity subsequences have the same limit, so the full sequence of <partial sums> converges and the sum is **zero**. This check is necessary: cancellation in fixed pairs alone does not always prove convergence of the original series.

For <absolute convergence>, each pair instead contributes
$$
|a_{2j}|+|a_{2j+1}|=2\ln\left(1+\frac1{2j}\right)
\geq\frac{2}{2j+1}.
$$
The inequality follows from $\ln(1+u)=\int_0^u(1+s)^{-1}ds\geq u/(1+u)$. The lower-bound series diverges by comparison with the <harmonic series>. Therefore the <paired logarithmic series with zero sum> has
$$
\boxed{\text{conditional convergence, but not absolute convergence}.}
$$

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <cylindrical coordinates> $x=\rho\cos\phi$, $y=\rho\sin\phi$. The <velocity field> becomes
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v=\alpha z\rho\,\widehat{\boldsymbol\phi},\qquad
v_\rho=v_z=0.}
$$
For each fixed height, particles follow concentric circular <streamlines> around the vertical axis. At $z>0$ the motion is anticlockwise viewed from above, while at $z<0$ it is clockwise. Within a horizontal plane the <angular velocity> is $\alpha z$, independent of <radius>, and the <speed> is $\alpha|z|\rho$. Both the plane $z=0$ and the vertical axis are stationary. The rotation rate varies with height, so the entire liquid is not one rigidly rotating body.

\Image[paper-1-height-dependent-rotation.png]
{title=Circular flow at fixed heights reverses direction across the stationary middle plane}

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a sufficiently smooth <vector field> on a volume $V$ and its closed boundary $S$, the <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\int_S\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf n\,dS
=\int_V\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf v\,dV,}
$$
with the <unit normal> directed outwards. Here
$$
\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf v
=\partial_x(-\alpha yz)+\partial_y(\alpha xz)+\partial_z0=0.
$$
Hence **the net volume <flux> through every <closed surface> in the liquid is zero**. This verifies <incompressible flow> directly. It does not require the <velocity> to vanish pointwise on an arbitrary <closed surface>; inward and outward contributions can cancel.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <curl> gives the <vorticity>:
$$
\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v
=\bigl(-\partial_z(\alpha xz),\ \partial_z(-\alpha yz),\
\partial_x(\alpha xz)-\partial_y(-\alpha yz)\bigr)
=(-\alpha x,-\alpha y,2\alpha z).
$$
Since $x\mathbf i+y\mathbf j=\rho\widehat{\boldsymbol\rho}$, the requested form is
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v
=-\alpha\rho\widehat{\boldsymbol\rho}+2\alpha z\mathbf k.}
$$
At the axis the radial <unit vector> is undefined, but its coefficient vanishes and the Cartesian expression remains smooth. The radial <vorticity> reflects the change in rotation rate with height.

= d
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Separate the closed cylinder into its curved side and its two end disks, all with outward <normal vectors>. On the side, $\rho=R$ and $\mathbf n=\widehat{\boldsymbol\rho}$, so
$$
\Phi_{\rm side}=(-\alpha R)(2\pi Rh)=-2\pi\alpha R^2h.
$$
At the top, $z=h/2$ and $\mathbf n=\mathbf k$, giving $(\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v)\cdot\mathbf n=\alpha h$. At the bottom, both $z$ and the axial <normal vector> change sign, so the same positive value $\alpha h$ results. Each disk therefore contributes $\pi\alpha R^2h$. Adding all three <surface integrals>,
$$
\boxed{\int_S(\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v)\cdot d\mathbf S
=-2\pi\alpha R^2h+\pi\alpha R^2h+\pi\alpha R^2h=0.}
$$
This explicit cancellation is consistent with the fact that the <divergence of a curl is zero>. The two disk contributions add rather than cancel.

= e
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For an oriented surface $S'$ with compatible boundary direction, the <Stokes theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial S'}\mathbf v\cdot d\mathbf r
=\int_{S'}(\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v)\cdot\mathbf n\,dS.}
$$
Let $H$ denote the removed patch and $S'=S\setminus H$ the remaining cylinder. Keep the outward orientation inherited from the closed cylinder. Everywhere on the removed curved patch, the normal <curl> component is $-\alpha R$, so its <flux> is $-\alpha RA$, where $A$ is its actual curved area. Part (d) then gives
$$
\int_{S'}(\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v)\cdot\mathbf n\,dS
=0-(-\alpha RA)=\alpha RA.
$$
Consequently the <circulation around a hole in an oriented surface> is
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial S'}\mathbf v\cdot d\mathbf r=\alpha RA.}
$$
The positive direction here is induced by the **remaining** outward-oriented surface; locally it runs clockwise around the hole when viewed from outside. If the edge is instead traversed positively around the **removed** patch, the direction reverses and the answer is **$-\alpha RA$**. The question does not specify an edge direction, so the sign must be accompanied by this convention. The result depends only on the removed curved area, not on its shape. If the patch has several boundary components, use the sum of their induced <line integrals>. Since $[\alpha]=1/(\text{length}\cdot\text{time})$, the result has the units of a <velocity> <line integral>, length squared per time.
